<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology in Education</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Use of Technology in School</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Interpret spoken advice on common problems. Interactive: Respond to questions in group discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can interpret spoken advice to understand limits on technology use (e.g., "I think this app is helpful because it makes it easy to access our homework and assignments, but I try not to use it too much so I don't get distracted by my phone."). Can respond to questions in group discussions by sharing opinions on technology use (e.g., "I love this app because it has fun videos."). Can respond in discussions by giving examples of useful or distracting apps (e.g., "In the audio, it is clear that video apps distract students during class.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of different technology devices (smartphones, tablets, laptops) and school settings. Students are asked to briefly share how they use these devices in school or at home for learning. The teacher prompts with questions like, "What apps do you use for school?" or "Do you think technology is useful for education?" to activate prior knowledge and introduce the theme. (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 8-10 key vocabulary words related to the theme: <i>technology, apps, benefit, problem, solution, use, ignore, appropriate, useful, limit</i>. The teacher says each word clearly, highlighting stress in multisyllabic words like "tech<b>no</b>logy," "edu<b>ca</b>tion," "inno<b>va</b>tive," and models intonation patterns for expressing opinions and suggestions. For example, "I <b>think</b> technology is really <b>useful</b>." or "You <b>should</b> limit your screen time." The teacher writes the words on the board and gestures to clarify meaning (e.g., mime 'ignore' or point to a 'useful' app). The teacher also practices minimal pairs focusing on /ʊ/ in "should" and /uː" in "shoot" to differentiate similar sounds. (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary and concepts. (5 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a phone helps you study, is it a <b>benefit</b> or a <b>problem</b>?" (Expected answer: "A benefit.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean to <b>limit</b> your screen time? Should you do it more or less?" (Expected answer: "It means to control or reduce it. I should do it more.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology in Education".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a short conversation (approx. 1-1.5 minutes) between two high school students discussing their opinions on using technology in school, common problems they face, and suggestions for appropriate use. The genre is an informal dialogue, focusing on opinions and advice. The dialogue incorporates simple present, "should," "can," Wh-questions, and the target vocabulary (<i>technology, apps, benefit, problem, solution, use, ignore, appropriate, useful, limit</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The students will interpret spoken advice and identify opinions on technology use in a school context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> (10 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pre-listening (Gist):</b> The teacher asks students to listen for the main idea. "What are the two students talking about?" (Listen for general topic: "Technology in school.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>While-listening (Specific Information):</b> The teacher plays the audio twice.</li>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">First listen: Students identify if the statements are True or False. (e.g., "Student A thinks all apps are useful for school." / "Student B believes technology can be a problem sometimes.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Second listen: Students match problems mentioned in the audio (e.g., "getting distracted," "too much screen time") with suggested solutions (e.g., "set limits," "use apps for studying instead of games").</li>
                    </ul>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for and identifying opinions, suggestions (using "should," "can"), and Wh-questions in spoken English. They focus on recognizing the target vocabulary and grammatical structures within the audio context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> (10 min)</p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Listening for Gaps):</b> The teacher provides a transcript of the audio with blanks for 5-7 target vocabulary words and key phrases using "should" or "can." Students listen to the audio a third time and fill in the missing words/phrases. Example: "I think this ______ is helpful because it makes it easy to access our homework, but you ______ use it too much." (Expected answers: app, shouldn't). (4 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Matching Advice):</b> The teacher provides a list of common technology problems (e.g., "Students get distracted by games," "Too much screen time is a problem"). Students listen to specific parts of the audio again and match the problems with the advice given by the speakers, writing down the full suggestion using "should." Example: "Problem: Students get distracted by games. Advice: You <b>should</b> ignore entertainment apps during class." (3 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Sharing What They Heard):</b> In pairs, students compare their answers from the previous activities, discussing any discrepancies. They verbally clarify what they heard, using phrases like, "I heard him say 'benefit,' not 'problem'," or "She said 'should,' not 'can' for the solution." (3 min)</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology in Education".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group Discussion. Students work in groups of three to five. The teacher presents scenarios related to "The Use of Technology in School" (e.g., "Your school wants to use tablets in every class, but some students are always distracted," or "Your classmate uses a translation app during English class to avoid listening to the teacher").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Each group member takes turns sharing their opinions and offering advice, building on what they heard in the audio and using the target vocabulary and grammar. They must actively *listen* to their peers' suggestions and respond with their own perspectives. For example, "I agree that we <b>should</b> set limits," or "What do you think is the best <b>solution</b> for distraction?" (10 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students practice weighing benefits and drawbacks: "Using tablets in school helps us access information easily, but it can be distracting."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They suggest limits: "We <b>should</b> have tech-free times at lunch so we can talk with each other more."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They express opinions and compare preferences: "I like technology in school. I think it's better for learning than traditional methods."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They engage in conversations on the appropriate use of technology, offering suggestions for limiting or incorporating it in school.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist during the group discussion to assess students' listening comprehension and their ability to respond interactively, using target language appropriately. The checklist focuses on active listening cues, relevant contributions, and use of specific vocabulary/grammar.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in a brief whole-class Q&A session where the teacher asks questions directly related to the audio and the group discussion topics (e.g., "What was one <b>problem</b> mentioned in the audio?" "What <b>solution</b> did your group propose for distracted students?"). Students must actively listen to the question and provide a coherent answer, demonstrating understanding of the topic and use of target language. (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active Listening: Demonstrates understanding of questions and peers' contributions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of Target Vocabulary: Incorporates words like <i>technology, apps, benefit, problem, solution, use, ignore, appropriate, useful, limit</i>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammatical Accuracy: Correct use of simple present, "should," "can," and Wh-questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and Coherence: Responses are easy to understand and relevant to the discussion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation: Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary and multisyllabic words.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts: (3 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List one new word or phrase you learned today about technology."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one piece of advice you heard in the audio or discussion that you think is most useful?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b> (2 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did I understand the spoken advice and opinions today? What was easy or difficult?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did I use new vocabulary like 'benefit' or 'limit' when discussing with my classmates?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The listening activities were effective in introducing new vocabulary and concepts related to technology use in school. Students demonstrated good initial comprehension of general ideas and some specific details from the audio. The group discussions allowed them to practice active listening and responding, integrating the target grammar and vocabulary. Some students struggled with the pronunciation of multisyllabic words and consistently using "should" for suggestions. Next time, I need to provide more explicit pronunciation practice for these words and structures. Additionally, some students needed more scaffolding to generate their own opinions and advice using the target language. I should prepare sentence starters or a word bank for the discussion phase to support lower-proficiency learners.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core vocabulary and concepts through listening. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will reinforce these same vocabulary words and ideas by having students read a short text about technology in education, building on their auditory understanding and further contextualizing the language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>